# Contributing to {{ project_name }}

## Setup

```bash
cp .env.example .env
uv sync
docker compose up -d db redis
uv run alembic upgrade head
uv tool install prek
prek install -t pre-commit -t commit-msg -t pre-push
```

## Making a change

1. Create a branch off `main`.
2. Make your change, keeping it scoped to one concern.
3. Run the checks locally before pushing:

   ```bash
   uv run ruff check .
   uv run ruff format .
   uv run pyright
   uv run pytest
   ```

   (`prek run --all-files` runs the same checks via the installed git hooks.)
4. Commit using [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/)
   (`feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `chore:`, ...) — the `commit-msg` hook and CI
   both enforce this, and `.github/workflows/release.yml` uses commit types
   to compute the next version and `CHANGELOG.md` entry on merge to `main`.
5. Open a pull request. CI runs lint, format check, type check, and the test
   suite against real Postgres/Redis service containers.

## Code style

- Google-style docstrings with explicit types on public functions.
- Keep request handlers non-blocking; move long-running work to the
  configured queue backend (see `database/queues.py`).
- Extend the service container in `bootstrap.py`, not ad hoc globals.
